  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and well above normal conditions will \ncontinue through Sunday.\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic will shift\nacross the Northeast/New England this weekend, winds will become\nmore southerly which will act to limit any lake influences. \nAdditionally, downsloping flow in combination with 850mb \ntemperatures peaking above the climatological 90th percentile \nfor mid-November, will support high temperatures Saturday \nafternoon a good 15 to 20 degrees above average (approaching\nrecords in some spots). The warm weather will then persist \nthrough Sunday ahead of the upper level pattern shift beginning \nearly next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much cooler conditions are expected next week, \nwith some chances for light rain and/or snow.\n\nThe mid to upper level pattern late this weekend and into the \nearliest portions of next week will feature a trough overspreading \nthe region from the north-central United States Sunday to the \neastern Great Lakes through Tuesday, where a ridge will then build \neast Tuesday night through Wednesday. Meanwhile at the surface, a \ncold front will sweep east across western and north-central New York \nSunday night, with surface high pressure gradually shifting east \nacross north-central United States and into the Great Lakes Monday \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nGetting more into the specifics, with the frontal passage Sunday \nnight into Monday morning, scattered rain showers will \noverspread the region from west to east due to low-level \nconvergence and moist ascent along the cold front and moist \nisentropic lift ahead of the trough axis aloft, but precip\namounts will be very light. In the wake of the frontal passage,\na colder airmass will filter across the region Monday. While a \ndry weather is expected to return, the post-front airmass should\nbe cold and moist enough to support lake-effect rain and/or \nsnow showers southeast and south of the eastern Great Lakes \nMonday into Tuesday. Any lake- effect precipitation should end \nby Wednesday as a lowering subsidence inversion and continued \nlow- level dry air advection cause lake- induced instability to \nwane.",
